findings.body:
`PluginHandle<R>` implements `Clone` by hand but is **not** `Copy` (see `crates/tauri/src/plugin.rs:124-136`). Dereferencing `handle_ref` therefore tries to move the value out of a shared reference:

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/app/plugin.rs" lines="135-143"}

`rustc` rejects this with `E0507: cannot move out of *handle_ref which is behind a shared reference`. The block sits behind `#[cfg(target_os = "android")]`, so desktop CI won't catch it. Every Android build of `tauri` would break.

The `handle_ref` indirection does nothing useful. Pass the handle straight in, the way `path/plugin.rs:239-240` does:

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri/src/app/plugin.rs" lines="138-140"}
        let handle = _api.register_android_plugin("app.tauri", "AppPlugin")?;
        _app.manage(AppPlugin(handle));

The whole point of this PR is to have `AppPlugin` own back navigation, so JS can intercept it or fall back to the default. The wry bump to 0.53.4 exists to get the `handleBackNavigation` switch, but `TauriActivity` sets it to `true`:

::diff{file="crates/tauri/mobile/android-codegen/TauriActivity.kt" lines="13-15"}

When this flag is `true`, `WryActivity` keeps doing its own "go back in webview history" handling, which is wry's existing default. That handling runs before or alongside the dispatcher callback `AppPlugin` registers. The result is that a JS `onBackButtonPress` listener can be bypassed while the webview has history, which is exactly when apps want to intercept. At best, both handlers race. Overriding the flag with its default value also makes the override pointless, which is a strong sign the intended value is `false`. I couldn't inspect the wry 0.53.4 source in this environment, so please confirm the exact semantics. The intent is clear either way: `AppPlugin` should be the only handler.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri/mobile/android-codegen/TauriActivity.kt" lines="15-15"}
  override val handleBackNavigation: Boolean = false

The JSDoc is just "Listens to the backButton event on Android." and an empty `@param handler`:

::diff{file="packages/api/src/app.ts" lines="263-275"}

Two things are left out. First, on desktop and iOS there is no `register_listener` command for `app`, so the returned promise rejects. Second, registering a listener disables the default back behaviour (`goBack`/close), and the handler then owns navigation, including calling `history.back()` when `payload.canGoBack` is true. Both points need to be in the public docs, ideally with a short `@example` and `@since`, matching the other exports in this file.

With no listener and no webview history, the callback briefly sets `isEnabled = false`, calls `activity.onBackPressed()`, and re-enables itself. This is the recommended AndroidX way to delegate to the next handler (or the system default) without recursing into itself, and it mirrors the Capacitor reference implementation.

::code{file="crates/tauri/mobile/android/src/main/java/app/tauri/AppPlugin.kt" lines="30-37"}

The `hasListener` helper added to `Plugin.kt` is also a small, reusable addition other plugins can use.
